CHAPTER 3

I was blinded by the foggy lights. Nothing was where it should be. My flames burned my own skin as the unknown voices yelled from beyond the shadows. I shielded my eyes only to be drenched in water and dragged
through the snow. The shadowy figure had reached me, red in its eyes. My wrist bleeds in his grasp. Helpless, I kick and grab the snow trying to resist. I yell for Adva, Vael, and my father, only to be met by 
laughter from those who drag me. A pain pierces the back of my head as I collapse to the ground, nose hitting the concrete beneath the snow. 

My chest tenses as my flames retreat into my body, leaving me defenseless. I try to strike back, making a small fireball and shoving it in their face— they laugh again. I scream for help, the falling snow
peacefully mocking me. Every voice spoke in the code I could not understand. They are going to kill me. I have to do something.

I bite down on their flesh, eventually freeing myself from their grip. I crawl in the snow, the freezing temperature piercing my fingers like needles. Tears well in my eyes. It’s only now I realize there’s not
a single elemental here. No tent, no soul, not even any lingering element from the celebration last night. Just me, and these monsters.

The shadow steps on my hand. Their shallow breathing sinks into my mind as my body freezes, rejecting my commands. My flames ignore me, and my body cools. Why is it allowing this? When I needed them most, they
retreated. My own flames leave me to die. 

I scream. “Please, stop!” tears roll down my cheek as I kick and scratch. Their hands cover my body as they try to pin me to the snow. They strike me again, my neck aching from holding it steady. I shiver, if
from the freezing cold or the paralyzing fear, I can’t tell.

Suddenly, their lights blind me again. I can’t see, but it all goes silent. My chest pinches my lungs, making it hard to breathe. I quiver and shake, waiting for the light to retreat. I’m not ready to face
this. I can’t, I don’t know what to do. 

My eyes shoot open. I sit up in my bed, flames flaring in fear. Half the tent is on fire, and yet Vael and Adva kneel in front of me, eyes wide. 

“Are you okay?” Vael says quickly, shooting up to me extending his hand. Adva exhales, collapsing her hands onto her knees. 

My eyes dart around the tent. My flames slowly devour the tent, the temperature rising enough to make Vael’s snow begin to melt. Adva stands, grabbing his wrist. “You need to go, Vael, you’ll—”

“I’m not leaving.” He barked, snapping his head over to Adva. Adva flinched, then her eyebrows furrowed. He turned slowly back to me, eyes locked onto mine, waiting for my response. 

I hide a smile, turning my gaze away from him towards the ground. “I’m okay. Just a nightmare.” I finally utter softly. My flames curl inward as my heart rate finally eases. The fire on the tent slows as I 
do. I draw a small smile on my face, trying to reassure him.

“Okay, let’s go.” Vael reached for my wrist, but recoiled at the heat. He paused for a second, as if he was trying to process something. Adva snickered. 

My smile grows. “You should go, Vael. You’re melting.” 

“I am?” He looks down at himself, water drips down his shirt and arms. “I am… Huh.” 

“Please, go. Before you melt…” I say, getting up. Adva tugs him out, laughing to herself. 

I don’t follow them immediately. I turn to look at the flames I caused. I could’ve seriously hurt them, and I ruined that lady’s hard work. I sigh. How many times is this going to happen? I don’t think I
could forgive myself if Adva or Vael got hurt. 

I checked to make sure my stuff was okay, digging under my bed. Sure enough, it was all perfectly fine. It’s all fire proof anyway, I was just hoping nothing fell onto it and singed it. I decided to take
them with me. I got lucky this time, but there’s no telling what’ll happen if I leave them behind. 

I stand at the tent flaps. It wasn’t ‘just a nightmare’. What if humans are actually like that? I don’t think I’m ready to defend myself, but I can’t remain helpless. If it came to it, I don’t think I’d 
trust myself to strike back. 

I finally emerge from the tent, welcomed by the gaze of multiple strangers. I tilt my head down to avoid the judgemental stares, trying to ignore their voices, while walking to hide being Adva and Vael. 

“This planet is too hot,” Vael whines. “Didn’t know that distance from the sun could make such a difference.”

“It looks much bigger in the sky, too.” Adva said. “And there’s no moon. The day sky feels weird without Kinnsi.” 

I look from in between their shoulders. She’s right— the sky’s missing something crucial. I can’t help but notice the sun is slightly bigger as well. My eyes begin to hurt looking at the sun for so long, 
so I snap my gaze away. It’s unusual, that never happens. 

Voices of elementals all around float through the camp. All kinds of beautiful tents made from ice, fire, stone, and flowers decorate the area. The energy feels stagnant, waiting for something to pop.
People look over their shoulders and watch the skies. It’s been too quiet for too long. 

Adva grabs my shoulder. “Come on, we’re going to find some food.”

I nod. “I think I remember seeing people setting up the food distribution over there last night.” I point, though my direction is blocked by multiple elementals who gather. Now that I think about it, I 
haven’t eaten in a while. Meals were too rare to dwell over hunger. Even in space, I refused half the meals I was offered. 

Vael nudges me. “Let’s go, then. Any longer and I might crumble!!” He tugs me along as he begins to march in the direction I pointed. I stumble, barely managing to keep my footing. I glance to Adva for 
help, but she just smiles as she begins to follow us. 

The crowd of elementals is tightly knit. It reminds me of the camp, as we have to weave through so many elementals all while I try not to burn them. Except this time, instead of being Adva, it’s Vael. 
It feels odd when I realize that it happened so long ago …

Vael eventually leads us to the line of the food distribution center. Around this time there’s usually not as many people here, but the line stretches far enough to reach the new gate the humans put up. 
I hear them talking in the mysterious tongue they use to communicate. It sends a shiver down my spine. I try to drown them out, but my curiosity keeps me focused on them. 

Adva joins us in the line, glancing at the humans. She stares at them for a moment, even holding eye contact with one. When the human points at her, still talking with his companions, she looks away. My 
heart skips a beat when he points at Vael and I as well. I snap my head so quickly, I feel my neck hurt a bit and sparks fly off my hair. 

I stare at the ground to avoid accidentally glancing at them again. Vael maneuvers himself closer to me, without saying anything. I look over at him, and he’s just staring forward, examining the group in
front of us. I feel a smile begin to creep up my face. 

“Those guys are weird,” Adva whispered. “They’re still staring at us.” 

Vael turns his head to look. The thought of locking eyes with them makes me feel ill. “Maybe they should mind their own damn business.” He says as he raises provocative signs on his hands towards them. 

“Vael, stop!!” I whisper, tugging his wrist. “You’ll provoke them.” 

He looks at me, something new shining in his eye. He moves himself in between my own gaze to the humans, blocking them from seeing me. His voice was lower, softer. “They won’t hurt you.” He uttered,
without looking at me. A shiver went down my spine. Does he know about the nightmare?...

My hand had slipped its way into Vael’s subconsciously. I didn’t even know how long it had been there, but I moved my hand back to my side slowly enough to try not to hurt his feelings. Adva’s snickering
from behind us echoes in my head. 

As we move in the line, Vael continuously positions himself so the humans nearby can’t see me. He constantly glances over at them, but never tells us what he’s seeing. I prefer it that way. If it were up
to me, they’d be gone and far away from the camp entirely. 

Adva stands next to me on the other side. I look over at her and she just smiles, as if trying to reassure me of something. I feel her Windsi curl on my leg, hugging it as we move forward in the line. 
The food’s smell growing closer forces me to confront how hungry I really am. 

“Hopefully they’ve got something left,” Adva jokes. “After all the people that have been through before us, I wouldn’t be surprised if it’s empty.”

“I hope they were smart enough to pack for everyone,” I responded, trying to peek to get a clue of how much food remained. Unfortunately, I’m too short, and too small. Vael shifted with me, still
standing between myself and the humans. My head brushed his arm slightly. “I can’t see from here.” I say, choosing to ignore Vael for my own sanity. 

“As always,” Adva says. Vael chuckles. “I’m sure we’ll be fine, I was just joking anyway.” 

The line continues to move forward. “There’s plenty left,” Vael adds. “I can see from here. We’ll be fine.” 

As we approach the tables, I notice humans entering the camp. My heart stops as I stare at them. Elementals nearby are forced to clear paths for them as they move further in, carrying large bags and
some of them have odd looking medium-sized metallic tools hanging off of their necks closer to their waists. Those with the metallic tools also have helmets on, matching the snow-white outfits they
are wearing. They comb through the camp, eyes darting to each elemental they pass.

Vael grabs my shoulder, turning me slowly. I look over at him. “Are you hungry?” He says, voice soft. He keeps his hand there, not allowing me to turn around. I nod steadily, still thinking about
those humans who just entered. Vael hands me an open pack of rations, a flavorless mixture of all kinds of Tulian vitamins. While flavorless, the smell is arousing, at least to me.

“Same food, different planet.” Adva says, taking a bite of her rations. Vael hasn’t grabbed any yet, instead giving me a utensil. 

“Eat.” He said softly as I took it out of his hands. Only after I took my first bite did he turn to get himself something. Then he turns back around, moving himself in between my own gaze and the
humans. He watches them closely, though.

Adva stays on the opposite side of me, also looking at them. She then turns to me. “How’s your food?” She asks, her voice soft. She faces me, but her eyes still look at the humans. What are they so
fixated on? I want to see, but at the same time, I don’t want to witness something I’ll never forget.

“It’s… as good as it can be.” I say, taking another bite. I face the ground, counting my steps. The energy feels like it’s on the edge. People look at the humans and voices quiet to whispers. I can
hear the clatter of their earthling equipment as they move around the camp. Yet I still don’t want to look. My ignorance will protect me, at least for now.

We walk together, closer than usual. Slower than usual. As if we were meant to walk like this. Like it was written in the stones of the Earth. The clatter of equipment grows closer by the second, 
and yet not a word is said. We continue to eat, moving slowly. Hoping to not be noticed. 

Suddenly, 6 humans stood before us, with more out of my view. Vael curled his arm around mine, interlocking us together. The humans look at us confused, speaking to each other and writing on flat 
tablet screens. Adva inches closer to us as well, wrapping her arm around my other one. One of the humans with the metal tools puts on large gloves. I glance between Adva and Vael, who stand straight
and stiff. Even Adva’s Windsi is motionless. 

The humans point between us, occasionally landing on our arms. The human with the gloves reaches at me, touching my hair and the top of my head. I flinch away, trying to avoid his touch, but my arms
being interlocked make it difficult to maneuver away. Eventually I push too hard, causing my arms to slip out and I fall onto my back. When Vael and Adva instinctually rush to help me, other humans
lightly restrain them by grabbing their shoulders and preventing them from walking forward.

“Stop!!”I yell, rushing to my feet to help them. I’m stopped too, however, when the man with gloves jumps in front of me, both hands up. I stare at him, an unknown swirl of emotions swimming through
my body. How dare they grab my friends like that? He reaches out to me again, slower than last time. I still avoid his touch, kicking away using the ground. At this point, he turns to his colleagues, 
speaking to them in their accursed tongue. 

Then, one of the humans reaches to me, recoiling at my rising heat. They raise their voices at each other, seeming to be growing frustrated. The man with gloves turns over to Adva, who’s fighting to
rush over to me, and the one behind me turns me around. I face her as she speaks to me slowly, as if it would help me understand her language. I turn my head slightly, seeing Vael resisting the humans
himself, before the woman catches my attention again.

Suddenly a person from behind me lays a hand upon my head. I jump, then freeze. I can feel the texture of the glove finding my scalp. They’re talking as the hands move further down, using my neck as
a bridge. I feel Adva’s Windsi try to wrap itself around my leg as her voice fades from behind me. My eyebrows furrow as the woman in front of me stops talking and begins writing. Are they studying me?

His hands continue to move, treating me as if I was some animal. I finally built the courage to move from his touch. I watch as their frustration grows, and now humans from behind grab  my arms. I 
kick the air and try to dig into the ground to make it harder to move. I prepare to raise my temperature to burn them off of me. They can’t just treat me like this.

Suddenly, a loud bang rings into the air. The camp falls totally silent, even the humans stop talking. The snow and dirt near my foot has flown into the sky, leaving a hole where it was hit. When I
look up, a human points the metallic tool at me. I can hardly see his face, but I can sense the malice from his gaze. I don’t dare to move a muscle. Humans holding me stick their hands up toward him,
and one even forcefully lowers his tool, moving his arms around quickly as he yells. Is that a weapon?

My entire body becomes solid. My flames heat up and the people touching me quickly move away, looking to their colleagues for help. If that thing hit me, I don’t think my flames could stop that. The
way the snow shot up, the sound it made… could that thing kill me?

I move my eyes, looking for Adva and Vael, but I can’t find them. The woman from before approaches me again, kneeling to match my eye level. Then, she begins speaking softly. I can’t stop shaking,
but I try to be as still as possible. I see the man who used the weapon staring at me. Like he’s waiting for me to mess up, eager to use it again. 

	The woman grabs my attention again, putting her hand in front of my eyes. She grabs my wrist slowly, wrapping a band around it. It automatically tightened, growing tight enough to remove my hand 
from circulation. Then it loosens slightly, giving my hand room to breathe, but leaving me unable to pull it off myself. 

The woman then pulls her tablet out again, holding her writing utensil in the same hand. She looks to her colleagues, who leave after she says something to them. A small smile appears across her 
face, as she sticks up two fingers on her free hand. My eyebrows furrow as I glance over, refusing to move my head. She taps her fingers with the pen, repeatedly.

“Two?” I whisper, after a short pause. 

“Two,” She repeats, trying to mimic my Firen. Then, in her language, she says it again. “Two.” 

She stares at me expectantly. She says it again in her language, eyes locked onto my soul.

“T.. two?” I repeat, trying my best to match her pronunciation, admittedly doing quite poorly. She nods, jotting down on her tablet. I look over her shoulder, seeing the man with the weapon still
there. Waiting. 

The woman clears her throat. Another distant bang rings out in the air, causing us both to jump. I freeze, but she turns quickly, looking for the source. After a few moments, she turns back to me,
still stiff. I hope that wasn’t meant for Vael or Adva. 

She rolls her eyes as she drops a finger. 

“One,” I utter in Firen, voice shaking. 

This time, she doesn’t mimic me. She moves her finger back and forth in front of my face. I glance between her and her finger, confused. She sighs, before resting her finger in between her own 
eyes, closer to herself. Then she moves it left and right, following with her eyes without moving her head. Then she rests it in between my eyes, and does it again— moving her finger left, right,
then left again. I follow with my eyes, without moving my head. 

After more quick scribbles in her tablet, she pulls a tool from her bag, much smaller than the weapon the man used. My anxiety still rises, however. Anything could be a weapon. 

She presses a button on the back, causing me to flinch. Instead of making a loud bang, it merely activates a foggy light. I look up at her, and she hardly acknowledges me, barring a gaze. Then, 
suddenly, she points the light directly into my eye, hurting it. I quickly snap my head away. 

She clears her throat, and I look back at her slowly. Her gaze is almost demeaning, as if she was disappointed in me. She lifts the light source again, this time close to my eye, though not directly
in it. Then she lowers her chin, widening her eyes as she stares at me. I’m afraid to move my gaze, so I return her stares, though without her energy. 

She slowly returns the light to my eye. I flinch, but hold. She moves the light around, examining it, before shifting to the other. She sits there for just as long. I don’t even know what she’s 
looking for.

She finally drops her light. The moment she does, I rub my eyes. Suddenly the band on my wrist sends a jolt through my flames. I yelp, and quickly stand straight again. I don’t know if it’s a warning
or punishment. The woman seems to laugh for a moment, before regaining her demeanor. I don’t move. I feel like I can’t. I consider burning her eyes, but decide against it. I don’t want to find out how 
those weapons feel.

She puts the light and tablet away. Then, she writes something on the band she put on my wrist using a different pen. Without another word, she walks off towards her colleagues. 

As soon as my chest loosens, I run looking for Vael or Adva. My heart races as the snow melts around my feet with each step. I hope they’re okay. 

I see Vael in the distance. A man with a tablet similar to the woman who was testing me is walking away from him. I waste no time hugging him, swinging my arms around him immediately, though part of
me still quivers from the tests.  

“Are you okay?” He whispers, rubbing my head. I don’t say anything. I just cling to him, and the world begins to feel less sharp. Everything slows down as his hand caresses my hair, as if his touch 
cleansed the human’s. My heart slows as he wraps his arm around me. Despite all this, my hands still shiver in anxiety. 

Once my mind relaxes, I quickly push away, staring at the ground to avoid his gaze. My flames flutter in the air as sparks fall from my cheeks.

He chuckles. “It’s okay. You were afraid.” 

His hand finally lowers from my scalp to my shoulder. I tilt my head up slightly, meeting his gaze. I want to say something, but I choke on my words. The bang rings in my head again. The snow and dirt
flying. The man's hateful gaze.

“Hey.” Vael says softly. I look up at him. He moves some of my hair from in front of my eye. “You’re safe now. Nothing will hurt you.” Something flutters in my throat as he speaks. I’m not confident I 
know what caused that reaction, but I turn my gaze away from him.

Adva appears from the treeline, her eyebrows furrowed as she stomps through the snow. Once she reaches us, I give her a hug as well. I feel her windsi zigzagging and stomping as she approaches, but it c
alms once we meet, wrapping itself around my ankle. 

“It’s official,” Adva mumbles as her hands glide slightly above my skin. “I hate humans. Are you alright?”

I nod. “I am, yes. How about yourself?”

“If anger doesn’t count, I am okay.” A tight smile grows on her face. “Vael? How about you?”

He takes a few steps towards us, closing the small distance. “I’m fine, yeah.” He tugs at the band on his wrist. It looks similar to mine, except it’s white. I then notice Adva has one as well, in a 
grayish color. Mine glows orange, reflecting the sunlight onto my skin. 

“Did you guys finish your food?” Vael asks, looking down at me with something shining in his eye.

“I rarely finish my food,” I say, turning my gaze to Adva. “It’s become a habit to save some.” 

“That’s a lot like you, honestly.” Vael responded, still tugging at his band. 

I tilt my head, returning my gaze to him. “What’s that supposed to mean?”

“Nothing bad, I assure you.” A small smirk appeared on his face. 

I considered speaking further on the topic, but I let it go. The camp has become deathly silent, as if everyone is afraid to move. I turned to watch as humans combed through the camp, doing similar 
tests on other youth that they did to me. The weapons are raised and pointed at anyone who dares be even slightly disobedient. I’m stuck waiting for the next bang to strike the camp again. 

I feel a hand on my shoulder. “You’ll panic if you keep thinking about it,” Adva’s voice called. Her hand squeezed my shoulder. “The last thing you need is more stress.” 

I turn to face her, Vael nodding in agreement behind her. When I look over at him, I realize something— we still don’t know his real name. My curiosity grows, but I shove the thought to the back of my 
mind. I don’t know how he’d react to me asking, and I don’t want to find out. 

He notices me staring at him, giving me a small, reassuring smile. I can’t help but wonder what it could be. I analyze his face, trying to think of any Snai name that I know— he kind of looks like a 
Sahlo. Or maybe a Ta’ei. 

Vael tilts his head, his smile growing genuine. I shift my gaze away… my eyes were fixed on him for longer than I’d like to admit. I hear Adva’s snicker from behind me as she begins to pat my head. The 
silence with us was different from the camp. I felt my chest warm as their presence behind me scared my anxiety away. I can’t help but giggle with Adva’s snicker, but I can’t help but search the camp for
any weapon-wielding humans who may be approaching us. 

Vael sits against a nearby tree. “You know, I,” he paused. “... never thought I’d make friends again. With my whole experience in the war, and…”

He goes silent. “... and?” I ask softly, sitting next to him. Adva kneels directly in front of him. My chest tightens as I brace for his next words. 

His gaze stays locked onto the ground. Snow around us begins to rise, clinging to him. “... I’m, uh.” another pause. He sniffles. I glance over at Adva, whose jaw tightens with each word Vael speaks.
Her fingers brush against her temple, resting on her cheek. Her windsi is almost too still, but I feel its light breeze on the opposite side of Vael to me. “I’m happy I met you guys.” Vael finally says,
voice low and shaky. “Thank you for bringing me …”

“Oh, Vael,” Adva begins, placing her hand on his shoulder. “It happened on its own. That means it’s destiny. You were meant to join us, for whatever reason that may be we can only wait to find out.” 
I feel her windsi hug Vael, wrapping around his arm. 

Vael chuckles. “I honestly thought you guys were scary when we first met,” he says. He reaches over to pet Adva’s invisible Windsi. I can’t tell if he’s actually petting it, or just sliding his palm 
over the air. “You especially, Imbra. You know, not seeing a Firen elemental and all…”

“Oh,” I recoil. “Uhm, sorry.”

“No, no. That’s not a bad thing.” He turns his body to face me fully. “Just.. my own lack of experience. You’re incredible.”

His words make me choke on my own, as my mind seems to burn everything to fixate on his compliment. The ends of my flames shine bright as my heat rises. I look away, staring at the ground, accepting
my failure to reply.

“She means thank you,” Adva says, sitting fully now. “Imbra’s not good at taking compliments.”

“I see that.” Vael replied, silently laughing.

The sound of footsteps in the snow from the other side of the tree catches all of our attention. We dart up, staring towards the source of the sound— a human female, who froze at our quick movement.
She’s accompanied by two men, one nicely dressed, and one with the weapon.

We exchanged glances in a delicate silence. “I’m peaceful, I promise.” The woman said, in Firen. “I’m not here to hurt, or test you at all.” 

Vael, Adva, and I exchanged glances, making sure we all heard the same thing. “You… speak Firen?” I ask. Even though I was talking to her, my eyes remained deadset on the weapon. 

The woman nods. “Yes, I do. I’m an official Elemental Specialist in the Global Government. May I get your names?”

Silence. Our eyes are locked on them. Every twitch or shiver draws our attention. A new silence, only interrupted by a stray breeze, hangs in the balance. The human waits patiently, a small smile 
growing on her face as she watches us.

“I can go first,” the woman begins, breaking the quiet. She took a small step forward, causing Adva to raise her fists. The woman bowed slightly, before saying “My name is Amei. Amei Korlann.” 

The silence hangs again, only interrupted by leaves swaying in the wind. The man in the suit speaks up in their human language, but Amei raises her hand, leaving her gaze upon us as she silences 
him. 

“... Adva.” She calls from behind me. “My name’s Adva.” 

Amei smiles. “Adva. I’ve never heard that one.” 

“Imbra.” I utter. My eyes are still fixated on the weapon, but I feel Amei’s gaze shift to me. 

She nods. “And what about you, Snai nascent?” 

I look over to Vael, watching as thoughts swim behind his eyes. I could only imagine what they are. “Vael.” He says softly, his voice breaking slightly. I was hoping he’d give his actual name.

“Adva, Imbra, and Vael.” Amei repeats it once. She pointed at us as she said our names. “It’s nice to meet you all!”

“What do you want?” Adva asks, speaking for all three of us.

Amei’s smile had only grown since she first approached us. “Well, I’m the one who’s in charge of watching you three.”

“Watching us?” Vael’s eyes narrow. 

Amei nods, glancing between us three. She must have noticed my eyes on the weapon, because she reaches over to push it, causing it to swing around the wielder's neck, landing behind him and out of
our view. “I promise, not the weird kind of watching. Let me rephrase-  I’m in charge of welcoming you to Gaea.” 

Not another word is spoken from us. I fear saying the wrong thing may provoke them to use the weapon. Amei seems nice, but it could all be a facade. “I know it may be hard to trust me, but you 
hardly have other options.” 

I want to say she’s wrong, but as I consider, she’s definitely right. Plenty of elementals in the camp left overnight, flying off to unknown fates. The camp will disperse, and we’ll be left to the 
evils of this encoded world. 

Adva takes a step forward. “Fine.” Her gaze conveniently avoids Amei’s. She must’ve been thinking exactly what I thought. 

The suited man speaks again, and Amei ignores him. “Wonderful!” She sighs lightly as her voice softens. “First order of business: moving you to your new home!” 

Vael and I lock eyes. I can feel his skepticism and anxiety through his gaze, reassuring my own. We’re taking a gamble with this human all to avoid taking one with our lives, but a gamble with this 
human is a gamble with our lives. We’re stuck.

“... No. First we set some rules.” Adva says, her voice stern and commanding. “If we’re coming with you, we need autonomy.”

Amei talks to the suited man, but for only a moment. They exchange a few sentences before she returns her gaze to us. “Of course. Lay out your rules.”

Adva glances at Vael and I, as if she read our minds for what we desire. “One, Only you are allowed to watch us. No human around without you present.” Amei nods. “Two, you will not watch us all day,
every day. We deserve privacy. Three, we are not test subjects. We will not provide any biological substances for examination.”

Amei had been nodding the whole way through. “Anything else?” 

“No weapons around us.” I say quickly, glancing at the man with one behind his back.

Amei turns to him, saying a few words. His face seems hesitant, but after a short back and forth, he walks away. “I can get that done.”

We sit in silence for a few moments, as she waits for us to speak up further. “If that’s all, it’s best we get moving now.” She glances down at a small clock on her wrist. “We’re on a schedule.”
She begins walking with the suited man. We follow.

“Are we sure we can trust her?” Vael whispers.

“No,” Adva whispers back. “But what other options do we have?” 

As we emerge from the treeline back into the main camp, gazes from curious elementals lock onto us. I can’t help but notice we’re the only three with these bands on our wrists. It hardly moves when
I tug at it. It seems to have tightened around my wrist perfectly, matching its shape. I hope they’ve got a plan to get it off. 

Once we exit the camp, the elemental eyes are replaced by human eyes. Hundreds stand outside with large cameras, all pointed towards us. Their foggy lights flash through the air for short moments,
blinding me. I reach for Vael’s arm to stay on path, looking downward to avoid the flashes. 

I kept my head tilted downwards. Each flashing light was accompanied by a loud click, intercepting other noises and making it difficult to hear. I raise my hand to block the flashes, only for my 
head to suddenly knock against a low-hanging metal frame. 

Vael turns, grabbing my wrist. “Careful-” he said softly, as he guided me through the obstacle. “Lift your feet… it’s elevated.”

Following his instructions leaves me kneeling in a small, cramped space. Adva’s taken a seat, with Amei and the suited man sitting opposite her. I sit next to Adva, fearing that I don’t have enough 
time to question what I had just entered. Vael sits next to me.

After a few human words from Amei, two doors slide shut. The force sends vibrations through the box and the sound sends a jolt through my body. 

“I apologize,” Amei says softly. “The doors are quite loud. I should have warned.” 

“What is this?” I ask, my hands gripping the soft cushions of the seat as I look around. My chest tenses as I notice the few options to escape.

Amei knocks on a small, thin window next to her. I can see another human on the other side. “Ah, this is called a vehicle. We humans can’t fly, so we built things to help us get around our planet 
easier.” 

Right as she finishes speaking, the vehicle jerks, beginning to move slowly. I nearly fall, having to catch myself on Adva’s shoulder. The vehicle hums as it moves, a small vibration flows throughout
the entire chassis. My hands clench against the cushion as my heart rate rises. I find myself blankly staring towards a wall, mentally preparing for a disaster.

The ride is almost entirely quiet. Adva keeps her focus near, but not directly on, Amei and the suited man, flinching at any and every sudden move they made. Vael stared toward the vehicle’s floor, 
tapping his feet occasionally as if he was attempting to make music. Amei only exchanged brief and quick conversations with the suited man in her human language, while I kept glancing around the entire
vehicle, every slight movement catching my eye. 

The vehicle comes to a gradual stop, the humming vanishing with it. Amei stands, walking past us and opening the doors, holding them for us. “There’s nobody out here this time.” She says with a 
small, painted smile. 

Vael exits first. “Thank you.” he says to Amei softly. I watch as she fights to keep her smile professional. 

I exited after him. “Thanks.” I smile weakly, a failed attempt to mimic her own. Adva exits behind me, repeating our gratitude. 

Amei closes the door, leading us forward. Each step crunches on the loose pebbles of the ground. Before us rests what I can only assume is another vehicle, with two massive wings spanning from
its body. 

Amei interacts with a tablet, tapping repeatedly whilst speaking in her human language. “What do you think it does?” Vael asks, whispering to me. 

“It flies, Vael.” I say. “Very clearly! Look at the wings.” I point to both the wings, my finger swinging to them both repeatedly. 

He blinks a few times, as if he was processing my answer. “How do they flap?” 

“I.. don’t think they do.” I respond, my gaze fixated on one. “Maybe they propel?” 

“I guess we’ll have to wait and see.” He nudges my shoulder with his, smiling softly. “Hope it actually works.” 

I giggle. “Why are they flying us if we could just fly ourselves?”

“To keep an eye on us, Imbra. Very clearly!” He responds, mocking my own tone. I punch his arm softly, and he laughs in response. 

Once we make it to the side of the vehicle, Amei stops. In her human language, she yells towards the vehicle, repeating the same phrase. Vael, Adva, and I are stuck waiting behind her, unsure of
how to proceed.

Amei turns to us. “Sorry.” She says softly, with a presentable smile on her face. She turns back to the vehicle, yelling even louder than before. Suddenly, a door from the vehicle’s body swings
down, presenting stairs as an entrance. Amei sighs heavily before turning to us again. “Let’s go.” 

She leads us up the stairs into a new, more open space, extending both ways from the entrance. Each pair of cushioned seats are accompanied by a table in-between, with four sets of them on both 
sides. At the end of both rooms rests a closed door, which color matches the wall, just with a bit more saturation. 

Amei turns to us again, this one quicker than the last time. “Okay-” she begins, speaking quickly. “I’ll be right back, make yourselves at home.” She weaves herself through us, entering the door
down the left hall. 

“How am I supposed to ‘make myself at home’ here?” Adva complains at a low enough volume that I almost don’t hear it. “This place is too confined.”

“You complain a lot, Adva.” Vael says. She gives him a glance that would kill him if her gazes could do harm. “Let’s just sit down and relax for once.” Adva mutters something under her breath. 

I nod in agreement. “Those seats look soft,” I say, pointing to the set closest to the right door. “Let’s sit there.” 

“See? Imbra agrees.” Vael snickers, leaning towards Adva with a daring smirk on his face. He grabs my hand softly and walks towards the seats I chose, holding his gaze on Adva as we pass. I hear
her growl before she follows us.

Vael offers me the seat closest to the window, gesturing me in as he caresses my hand with his thumb. I feel a warmth rise in my chest as a smile sneaks through, and I inch into the seat, leaning
towards the window. Vael sits next to me, with Adva sitting opposite of us, glaring out the window with her arms crossed. 

“Think she’s upset with me?” Vael whispers in my ear. 

I giggle. “Definitely.” 

He sighs, rolling his eyes with a small smile. “Advaaa.” he begins, leaning forward with his chin resting on his hands. “I’m so–”

“What’s your real name, friend?” Adva asks quickly, turning to face him. Her eyes are narrowed, nearly closed, as she tilts her head up.

 I flinch, quickly looking over to Vael, who chokes on his words. “I- I uh-”

“What? Can’t remember, or something?” A smirk grows on her face. “You know Imbra’s. You know mine. Yet we don’t know yours.”

“Adva!” I yell through gritted teeth. “Stop! It’s not that serious!”

“Then why can’t we know?” She leans forward. “I know you’ve been wondering, too.” 

I trip on my words, causing me to spew gibberish in my own confusion. Adva just chuckles slightly, before turning her gaze back to Vael, waiting expectantly. “Well- I…” He visibly lags, his mind
struggling to consider all the possible outcomes. 

Just in time, Amei approaches us. “There you guys are!” She says, a smile growing across her face. “I’m not supposed to leave you alone like that- glad you didn’t go running off.”

Silence. None of us respond to her, instead avoiding her gaze. “What? Did I do something wrong?”

“No,” Adva says softly, picking at her nails. “Just a bit of tension.” 

Amei sits beside her. “Is.. everything okay?”

Adva nods. “Yup. Nothing to worry about.”

Amei turns to Vael and I. “Imbra? Vael?”

“... Yeah,” Vael responds after a short pause. “Yeah. It’s all good.” I nod as he speaks.

“Okay then…” Amei sits up straight. “We’re about to start flying. It may feel a little odd at first, and you should stay seated during take-off. I’ll let you know when you’re clear to stand.
Okay?” Adva and I nod. Vael seems distracted, locked in his own mind. 

I attempt to comfort him by laying my hand upon his, giving it a few pats. He doesn’t look at me, but I feel his arm tense. 

“There should be two different metal boxes beside your thighs—” she pauses, taking a moment to grab hers, and giving us a moment to grab ours. I do, the cold metal sending a shiver back up my arm.
“Pull them across yourself tight, making them meet in the middle. Then insert the thinner one into the larger one’s slot, and keep pushing until you hear a click.” I hear hers click. When I try,
it easily slides in, a similar click to hers emitting from it as soon as I feel resistance. That felt pretty easy, I don’t think such an in-depth explanation was needed. 

I hear Adva’s click, then Vael’s shortly after. “Now keep them on until we’re fully in the sky. I’ll let you know when that is.” 

The vehicle jerks, the belt catching me before I can tumble again. I stare at the window, watching as the environment shifts from a large, open indoor space to a flat paved path. It rests for a 
few moments, before picking up speed near instantly, a similar yet louder hum coming from outside. The speed is quite slow compared to Elemental flying speeds, but the harsh acceleration catches
us off guard. I watch Adva clench the table and Vael cling to the seat, as I hug the wall. 

Suddenly, the vehicle tilts up, rising off the ground and into the sky. The land grows further and further away, before we pierce the clouds, the sunset greeting me through the window. The orange
and pink sky feels lackluster without Kinnsi. 

I hear another click coming from Amei’s belt as she stands. “This journey will not be short,” she says. “The door behind Vael has a set of beds for you to sleep on if you want to rest. If you need
me, there’s also a button in there to call for assistance. Big and red, hard to miss. Right now I have other business to attend to, but I'll be close by." With a wave, she walks off back to where
she came from.

Yet another delicate silence falls upon us. Adva’s got a look on her face that I just can’t pinpoint, something hovering between anxiety and pride. Vael’s got his head down, his back rising and
sinking with his breaths. Then there’s me, just here. For once, my mind is quiet. Watching the clouds flow by like the ocean brings a peace I never knew I needed. My chest loosens as my flames
dampen, and I begin to feel drowsy as the sight hypnotizes me. 

Slowly, the world quiets. The clouds shield my mind, and my eyelids grow heavy. I try to fight it, but my body forces me to accept it. Within seconds, I fall fast asleep, the world wishing me a
beautiful goodnight with the sunset blessing my eyes.

