— — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — CHAPTER 4 — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — —
I open my eyes, yet I still can’t see. The room was hardly illuminated by my own flames and a dim candle in a corner, where a figure sat still. I sit up, rustling and muffling the blankets placed over me to 
keep me warm. The figure’s head looks over at me, and I freeze. 

“Oh. You’re awake.” Vael’s voice called. He stood, picking up the candle and bringing it with him. I rubbed my eyes. “I didn’t wake you, did I?” His voice was soft and careful.

I shake my head. “I don’t think so—”

“Shh,” He keeps his same, low tone. “Adva’s asleep behind you.” 

I turn, seeing her motionless figure in the bed next to mine. “It’s okay,” I return my gaze to him. “She’s a deep sleeper, contrary to how she acts.” 

“Huh.” The flame on the candle’s wick dances beneath his chin. “She’s quite the wildcard, isn’t she?”

“Yeah, she definitely is.” I giggle a little, trying to hold it back to not make too much noise. “But in her own little way.”

Vael sits next to me, placing the candle in a slot on the nightstand. We sat in silence for a moment. “... We never finished our talk in the snow.”

I look up at him, a small grin growing on his face. “Well, what more was there to talk about?”

“I was interested in your life before the war.” His smile grows as he leans slightly backwards. “What you did, what you studied, stuff like that.”

I pause for a moment, my mind remembering the bliss and joy I felt back then. “Oh, where do I start?” I say, heat rising in my chest. 

“Well, how about your studies?” He inches closer to me. “You never told me what you wanted to learn at university.”

I look down at the ground, breaking our eye contact, recalling all my greatest moments. “I was studying the history of war and combat.” I close my eyes, trying to bring the memories to life. “Alongside a secondary
study of battle tactics and strategies.”

“You went to military school?!” Vael says, raising his voice while keeping his whisper. 

I nod. “I wanted to join the command structure of New Iris at some point, but…” I feel the warmth drain. 

“... I went to school for global politics.” He says after a short pause, placing his hand on my shoulder. “I wanted to lead Calen someday, so I took a lot of studies- economics, history of Calen, the whole book.” 

“That sounds stressful.” I say, looking up at him again.

He shakes his head softly, closing his eyes. “It was, very. But I knew what I wanted, and nothing was going to stop me from trying.” 

“That’s incredibly admirable, Vael.” 

“Thank you,” his hand finds its way on my head, caressing my hair. “Military school was my second choice. I also had aspirations to join Calen’s command structure…”

It’s only then I see it. The way my light reflects on his snow— highlighting every angle, every flaw, every glimmer in his face. His mouth moves, but my eyes are fixated on the shines and glows of his element. 

“... and that’s partially why I was so fascinated with you.” He says, finishing the sentence I missed. His fingers still caress my hair, feeling much different than it did at the camp. Now it feels perfect, pressing
my own warmth onto my scalp. My heartbeat slows as he stares back into my eyes. 

I had already begun leaning into him subconsciously, my body pressing against his. I placed my hands on his shoulders as the distance between us slowly closed. I felt his cold skin, a sharp contrast to my own, and
yet it felt right. 

His hand found its way to my chin, guiding me towards him. I wrap my arms around his neck, our eye contact only being broken by mismatched blinks. I bridge the gap, our lips finally connecting. His hands find their
way to my waist as the moment holds, only breaking after I pull away to take a breath. 

Our gazes meet again, his eyes glimmering something new. “...My name’s Kael.” He utters softly, refusing to break our eye contact.

“Kael…” I say, in between breaths. I consider speaking again, but I decide to lean in instead, pressing my lips against his once more. My hands flow through his hair as my heat rises and his temperature drops, further
blooming our perfect contrast. 

During it all, it begins to feel too open. My mind races with doubts. Have I gone too far? What if this makes everything awkward? What if I just lose him? I can’t give myself something else to lose. I can hardly
protect myself, how can I expect to protect my loved ones?

I pull away quickly, unable to control my breathing. “... Are you okay?” He whispers, his hand hovering near my face.

Every word I want to say tries to come out all at once, clogging my mind. I can hardly think of a full sentence as my thoughts are flooded by affection, desire, fear, and anxiety, all at once. Tears well in my eyes,
and all I can think of is to flee. 

I jump off of him, rushing to the door, closing it behind me. I run through the hallway and hide in a different door— a bathroom. Close, confined, and quiet. 

I immediately collapse into a ball, quivering while my breath and heart rate only quickens. I bury my face into my hands as my head pounds, the constant flow of thoughts making it impossible to think of anything else.
I kissed him. Our lips touched. We held each other. And it all felt so perfect. But I can’t risk losing him. I can’t risk him making mistakes to protect me. 

I don’t know how much time passed. Still curled into a ball, I hear a few soft knocks on the door. 

“Imbra?” Adva’s voice calls slowly. “I know you’re in there. This door’s smoldering.”

I don’t respond, my eyes locked onto the door, waiting for even the slightest movement. Still, when she opens it, I jump- the sliding door’s sound startling me. She peeks her head in, a worried smile on her face.
“Hey.” 

I just shake my head. Every time I want to speak, too many words come at the same time. My mind rushes all thoughts and outcomes at once, overwhelming my own consciousness. 

She nods. “Yes, I am coming in.” she says, responding to my gesture. She sits across the room from me, the heat already getting to her. “Va– err.. Kael told me what happened.” Her voice was soft as she leaned towards
me.

I turned away, my flames raging. Small fires had lit everything flammable in this room, but luckily the structure wasn’t, or I’d likely had fallen out of the vehicle by now. 

I felt Adva’s Windsi climb onto my head, rubbing it softly. Adva extended her hand towards me. “Let’s talk about it over breakfast. Amei brought us food, and you really need to eat more.” I look up at her. “Come on
, just like Tulia. Just me, you, and some food. The only difference is we’re not hiding under rubble.”

I can’t help but smile a little. My mind slows down as she speaks. “... Okay.” I say softly, my flames quieting with my thoughts. I reach my hand up for her Windsi, who hugs it.

Adva chuckles. “You know, she really likes you.” she says, standing to open the door for me.

“Why haven’t you named it?” I ask, standing to exit. My body still quivers slightly, but my anxiety is finally fleeting. 

“I have,” She responds, tilting her head and furrowing her eyebrows. “Have I never told you?”

I shake my head. “To be fair, I never asked.” 

“Her name’s Yrusei.” Adva follows me as I exit the room. “Named after someone I knew when I was really young… who died. It’s like there’s a piece of her with me wherever I go.” 

“That’s… amazing, Adva.” I say, tilting my head. Yrusei slips slightly, clinging onto my hand. “And it’s a wonderful name.” I feel her nibble on the tip of my finger softly. 

Adva leads me to a table, with two plates sitting opposite each other. She sits at the one further from us, leaving the closer one open for me. “It’s Tulian food.” she utters. “Not rations, either. Genuine, real
Tulian food.”

I sit across from her, and sure enough, there it is. A plate of Dalia, a Firen meal made with special spices, rare flowers, and delicate, soft meat from the Jursiu animal. To top it all off, it smells amazing. 

“Who made this?...” I ask, looking up at Adva.

“No idea. However, that will not stop me from enjoying this meal.” She began to eat her food, visibly entering a different world as her tongue processed the taste. 

I did the same. Munching on this food takes me back to before the war, when my mother would send me meals while I was in school. She’d put her heart and soul into every dish she made for me, and would leave little
notes for me to read, wishing me luck in my studies. 

Each bite calms my mind. If it weren’t for the environment, I’d believe we were on Tulia. Home feels closer with each piece I take. 

But it all ended too fast. It felt like I had just started eating, but it was all already gone. I definitely felt better, my stomach now full of necessary nutrients, but I wish it could’ve lasted longer. 

“Do you want to talk about it now?” Adva asks, leaning towards me. 

I look up at her, her face peaceful and welcoming. She doesn’t say anything further, her eyes send the message. “... I kissed him.” I say softly, looking down at the table.

“Well, he said he kissed you.” She tilts her head. “So, which one is it?”

“I kissed him.” I repeat, this time slightly louder. “Twice.” 

“Oh, twice?” She smirks. “You must’ve really enjoyed the first one then.” 

Heat rushes to my face as the flames on my head flourish. “What? No- I mean, yes, but-” I trip over my words, gibberish replacing my speech.

Adva giggles. “Didn’t mean to fluster you~” she lays her hands on the table in front of her. “Why’d you panic?”

I stare at her, considering if I should tell her or not. If I told her I was afraid of losing him, I wouldn’t want her to assume I don’t feel the same way about her. I do, it’s just… different with Kael. 

“I won’t make fun of you… maybe.” She says, a small smile on her face. She pushes her now empty plate to the side of the table, away from her elbows. 

“... Too many feelings, all at once.” I utter, looking down at my now empty plate. I didn’t want to lie to her, so I didn’t. I told her a reason, just not the reason.

“Ah. Classic Imbra.” She pauses. I feel her foot touch my shin, so I look up at her. “Would you kiss him again?” 

I freeze. I can’t guarantee the honesty of either answer. I blankly stare at her, processing the possibility of both outcomes.

“... You don’t know, do you?” She lays her hand on top of mine. I shake my head slowly, after a moment of thinking. “That’s okay. Don’t kill yourself thinking about it.” 

I look up at her, her soft, steady gaze welcoming my conclusion. “... Okay.” I respond softly. “Thank you, Adva.”

“Don’t thank me.” She leans back in her seat, a small smile growing on her face. “I’m just taking care of you… you’re basically all I have, after all.”

I tilt my head. “What about Kael? Yrusei?” 

“Kael’s not quite there yet.” I watch Adva’s gaze shift to something I cannot see. Yrusei stirs on the table right where Adva’s eyes land, her swirling causing stray flakes to rise off of it. “And Yrusei? She 
knows I love her.” 

Amei approaches us, her steps clicking against the ground as she walks. “Hey girls. How was your food?” 

“Too good…” I utter. “I’m upset it’s gone.” 

Amei sighs. “I suspected that would be the case. No worries, there’s more where we’re going.” She looks around the room. “Where’s Vael?” Hearing the old name feels odd now. My stomach tightens slightly in response. 

“In the bedroom.” Adva says without looking at her. “He’s fine.” She seems annoyed that Amei’s arrived.

She glances at the door to our bedroom. “Well, you need to prepare. We’re landing in about 20 minutes.”

I nod my head, but Adva doesn’t respond. Amei walks off towards the bedroom. “Still haven’t been told where we’re landing, by the way.” Adva mutters.

“I’m sure it’s nowhere too bad.” I say, trying to raise her spirits. 

“If humans will be there, it’s bad.” She doesn’t look at me. Yrusei suddenly stops all of her movements, sitting completely still. 

I sigh. “That’s a terrible outlook, Adva, considering we’re on the planet of the humans.” 

I hear Amei approaching from the bedroom. “That does not change my conclusion.” Adva says, quickly switching to Airren. 

I recoil. “Did you really switch because Amei’s walking by?” I respond in Airren. I haven’t spoken the language in a while, so my pronunciations are a little rough. 

She nods. “She’s spying on us, no doubt.” 

“I suspect you’re just paranoid.” Amei had walked by at this point. I noticed she gave us an odd glance when she realized we weren’t speaking Firen. 

“Paranoia kept us alive on Tulia.” She presses her index finger into the table, her eyebrows furrowing. “Never forget that.” Yrusei jerks, causing a breeze strong enough to nearly knock my plate off the table. 

“As much as I want it to be, this is not Tulia.” I lean towards her, chest tightening as I raise my voice. “The sooner we accept that, the easier things will be.” 

She recoils, leaning away from me. She exhales heavily, slowly turning her head towards me. “I just… I don't want this to be ruined.” Her voice is soft and lower. She looks away from me, staring down at the table.
“Anything could happen, then suddenly Gaea becomes just as hostile as Tulia.”

My body eases. I sit back, resting my arms. “I didn’t mean to yell, I’m sorry.” I utter. 

“No, I get it.” Her gaze returns to me. “I’m being very pessimistic. I imagine it’s not easy to deal with.” She returned to Firen halfway through her sentence. 

I reach my hand out to her. “We were molded to be that way.” I say, returning to Firen as well. “Just because I don’t say it, doesn’t mean I don’t think it.” 

She tilts her head, a smile cracking through as she laughs. “You’re a great talker, Imbra. I see why Kael’s into you.” 

“Oh- uh, thank you.” I say, my voice shaking. 

“Speaking of…” She smirks, leaning in. “Sister-to-sister. What about him attracts you? Hmm?”

I jump, flames rushing up my back. “Adva!!” I yell through gritted teeth.

“Shh. I won’t tell him. Don’t worry.” She climbs across the table, sitting directly next to me. “Come on. Tell me.” 

All the reasons come to mind at the same time, despite me trying to silence them. “... His skin’s soft.” I whisper. “And he’s really caring.” 

“There’s more though, isn’t there?” Adva giggles. 

I nod. “Way, way more.” It’s clear she knows— hiding it is pointless. “His eyes are wonderful… and he talks so well. And he’s funny—”

I heard footsteps approaching from the bedroom. My heart stopped while my entire body tensed. “Uhm— sorry, did I… uh...” Kael called. I could hear his voice shaking. When I looked over at him, his head snapped 
away from me, flicking everywhere but my direction.

Adva snickered. “Yes, Kael? Something you want to tell us?”

I curled into a ball, shoving my face into my knees. She totally did that on purpose. She knew he was coming. Flames rise all over my body as I hear Adva’s devious giggle. 

Kael clears his throat, reaching his hand behind his head. “Amei said we’re landing soon.” He said quickly, still staring towards the ground. 

“Wonderful news!” Adva jumps up, touching his shoulder. “Oh- your skin is soft. How much longer do we have?” 

He recoils at her touch, entire body quivering. “I… uh…” His voice shivers as he speaks. 

Adva gasps. “I’ve got an idea! You sit right here…” She grabs Kael and shoves him where she was just sitting— right next to me. “... And I’ll go find Amei. Don’t move, I’ll be right back!” 

“Adva, wait—” I say. Unsurprisingly, she ignores me as she runs off to find Amei. 

Kael and I don’t move a muscle. The silence is so tense, I feel like I could reach forward and grab it. I want to say something, but I don’t know what. I’m putting so much effort into keeping my flames on my b
ody, trying everything not to explode.

Ask something normal. Anything. What could possibly be normal right now? “Did… you eat?” I finally ask after a long pause. I still don’t look up at him. I feel him flinch when I start talking.

“Y-Yeah. Yeah, I did.” He clears his throat. “It was surprisingly delicious.” 

I nod, the stiffness in my neck causing my upper back to move with my head. Out of words again, with no clue what to say. It’s already awkward, but I fear anything else I say can only make that worse. 

Kael clears his throat again. “S-Sorry. I didn’t mean to pressure.. you…” 

“No, no you didn’t! I just– It was—” Gibberish conquers my speech again. No matter how hard I try, I keep tripping over my words. He glances at me a few times, his cheeks twitching as he fights a smile.

As if on cue, Amei walks up while Adva stands behind her, a large grin resting on her face. “We’re landing. Strap in.” Amei says quickly. Adva sits opposite us, fastening the belt around her. Kael and I do
the same. 

“Soo…” Adva begins as Amei walks off, back down the narrow hall she came through. “Have any conversations?” 

Kael slumps into his seatbelt, groaning. I crush my face into my hands. Adva just giggles. “I guess not.” After a pause, her eyes suddenly light up. “Oh, Imbra! Did I tell you how—”

Kael jumps up, only remaining seated thanks to the seatbelt. “Don’t! Please don’t!” He yells quickly, extending his hands across the table towards her.

She laughs. “What? I think she’d find it cute.”

“You promised earlier.” Kael whispered through gritted teeth. 

“Oh. I did, didn’t I?” Adva shrugs. “You’ll have to wait, Imbra.”

“Wait. But-” I catch myself mid sentence. I do want to know, but she promised she wouldn’t tell. But what’s so important that she had to promise that? 

Kael sighs. “Please, Adva.” 

She stares at him for a few moments, eyes scanning him. “Fine. I guess—”

The vehicle had finally touched the ground. Kael was halfway through his belt, and ended up jerking backwards, hitting his head on the cushioned seat. The roughness of the ground made the entire vehicle 
shake violently. I grabbed onto the table, which remained sturdy as ever through the chaos, trying to sit myself still.

The vehicle gradually slowed down, eventually coming to a total stop. I began panting almost instantly; I was holding my breath without realizing. I turn to Kael. “You okay?” I ask softly. 

He nods, rubbing the back of his head. “These cushions aren’t as soft as they look.”

Amei walks up again. Where does she keep going? “Come on. We’re leaving— and don’t worry about your stuff, it’s already where we’re going.” 

I had briefly wondered where my bag went, but I decided not to worry about it. We all stand, following Amei as she leads us, talking into her tablet. I can see a new face on it, who responds to her.
Unfortunately, I still can’t speak their language. I have noticed patterns, with words like “understood” and “yes”, but I have yet to fully know what they could mean. 

“I wonder where they’re taking us.” Adva says in Airren. 

Kael’s eyebrows furrow. “Hopefully somewhere warm.” I respond, using the same language. Kael squints his eyes. 

“Relax, guys.” He says in Firen. “I don’t speak Airren.” 

Adva looks up at him. “You’re gonna have to learn. That’s our new go-to language so Miss Amei over there can’t understand us.” She whispers, also in Firen. 

Kael pauses for a moment, before speaking in Nattai, one of the two Naturine languages I have yet to learn. Every word he speaks slips through my mind as untranslated gibberish. 

“You speak Nattai, but not Airren?” Adva says, a slight hint of annoyance in her voice. “Whatever. Guess we’re stuck with Firen.” 

Amei leads us to another vehicle, this one smaller than the last two. She disconnects with the person on her tablet, the face disappearing, before she turns back to us. “Okay.” She exhales. “Welcome to
Oceania. The Government here was oddly incredibly excited to take you as official inhabitants.” 

“Suspicious.” Adva whispers.

“I agree.” Amei nods slowly. “But don’t tell anyone I told you that. I’m not supposed to have opinions on things like these.” 

Kael tilts his head. “Howcome?”

“It’s a long, complicated story.” Amei says as she opens the door to the vehicle for us. “Maybe I’ll tell you someday.” 

We enter the vehicle, Amei closing the door behind me. Then she enters the front, starting it up. The vehicle hums and vibrates softly similar to the first one, except it’s far more compact and tight.
I grip the seats, trying my hardest not to burn anything. 

During the ride, I feel Kael lay his hand on top of mine. The temperature contrast takes me back to the kiss. How everything then felt so perfect. I think I would kiss him again if I knew I wouldn’t
lose him… but I can’t guarantee that. 

He keeps his hand there the entire ride, humming a soft tune. His hand squeezed mine a few times, sending his chilly temperature through my body. It stung a little at first, but it felt quite nice after
a while. 

I hadn’t even realized that the vehicle had stopped. Amei opens the door next to me. “We’re here- your new home. And there’s someone you guys have to meet. Come, come, hurry.” She beckons us towards her. 

We exit the car, her closing the door behind us. Her steps click against the concrete of the ground as she approaches the door of this new building, a white box-ish shape with a triangle roof. The box expands
outwards, leading to new shapes and even yards on the side. It looks quite luxurious for my Tulian taste, but a home is a home. 

Amei opens the door for us, revealing the living room. Before I could explore it, I couldn’t help but notice the Poisi Elemental sat on the couch. Yrusei wasn’t energetic on the way here, but she noticeably
went stiff once we acknowledged his presence. He had a tablet similar to Amei’s, speaking to it in the same, unknown language that Amei speaks. We watch them greet each other in the human language before
Amei turns to us. 

“Xor, this is Imbra, Vael, and Adva.” Amei says. She’s still calling him Vael… I wonder if I should correct her. “Imbra, Vael, Adva. This is Xor.” 

He stood from the couch, a small smile rising on his face. “Hello.” He said calmly, his voice raspy. Kael took a half-step forward, clipping me slightly out of Xor’s vision. None of us respond to him, but
all of us stare— waiting for any wrong move. He chuckles awkwardly. “A silent bunch?”

Amei rolls her lips inward slightly. “... Ease up, guys.” She says softly. “I promise, he’s not hostile.”

Xor nods. “I’m here to be a similarity.” His voice became robotic, almost as if he was rehearsing a line. “I’m the bridge between you and humanity.”

Not a single response. Not even Yrusei moves, I feel her standing frozen behind Kael’s leg. “Xor’s been on Gaea for 13 years now, and he’s built lots of trust with our governments.” Amei says after an 
expansive frame of silence. “We brought him here to help you integrate, because an Elemental would understand you better than any human could…”

I try to think of how to respond. Leaving my mind to run never usually ends well, and it doesn’t this time either. I remember Adva specifically stating no humans should observe us without Amei present,
but she never said anything about other Elementals. Is this their work around?

Adva sighs heavily, stepping forward. Her gaze is fixated onto the carpeted ground. “What purpose does he serve?” 

“He’s going to help you integrate.” Amei repeats. “And—”

“Why do we need to integrate?” Adva snaps her gaze up to Amei. “We can just be Elementals. We don’t need to be Gaeans.”

Amei smiles awkwardly. “I don’t mean to be hostile, dear, I-”

“New rule.” Adva’s hand shoots upward into the air, her index finger pointed up. “We are not integrating or accepting Gaean culture as our new ‘normal’. We are Tulians by heart, and it will remain that way.”

Amei and Xor exchange glances. I love Adva— she always manages to say what I’m thinking before I can even think it. I nod in agreement. “Honestly, I don’t want to be like humans. I want to be like my people.
I will keep my nation alive, even if I’m a few planets away…” 

When I say that, Amei looks at me with a sparkle in her eye. She tilts her head, choosing silence. “Okay, understood.” Xor says, taking Amei’s role. “You’ll still be Tulians. Gaea will not infringe on your culture.” 

“Then what are you here for?” Kael asked quickly.

“I will still serve as your bridge.” He knelt before us, despite being the same height as Kael. “To help you understand the world. You don’t have to integrate, but you should still know its systems.”

Adva and I look at each other. We nod once together in unison, and she turns back to Xor. “Okay. We can accept this.”

“Glad we could come to an agreement.” Xor says, standing to his feet. He begins interacting with his tablet again. “That changes things, though.”

“What things, exactly?” I ask sternly. 

Amei and Xor look at each other, as if silently deciding who should present the news. “It’s complicated,” Amei says softly. “But I think it’ll be worth it for you to hold onto your culture.”

“I think we should decide that.” Kael said, taking another half-step forward. I was almost completely shielded from Xor, only being able to see him by peeking around Kael’s shoulder. “It doesn’t matter
how complicated it is.”

Xor extends his hands. “Calm, nascent. It’s nothing major—”

“Major enough for you to hesitate.” Kael shakes his head while speaking. Yrusei swirls around his legs. “We came here to escape kneeling, we won’t be forced to do so.” 

Amei tries to hide a blooming smile, tilting her head down and away from us. Xor facepalms, a low groan coming from him. “I’m just the messenger, okay?” he looks at Kael again, lowering his hand. 
“I don’t make the systems. I enforce them.”

“But you know how they work.” Kael balls his fists, the snow on his body leaping. “That’s all I care about.”

Xor sticks his arm out. “Okay, relax. I’ll tell you.” His voice is soft and traced. “Just know I am on your side before Gaea’s. It’s—”

“Just get to it.” Kael’s begun tapping his foot on the ground rhythmically. 

Xor spreads his fingers, as if trying to feel the wind from Yrusei’s presence. “There will be curfews.” He begins. “Travel restrictions. More monitoring. Dietary enforcements, and…” Xor pauses
, looking over at Amei.

“And?” Kael says almost immediately after Xor pauses. 

“... You’ll have to hide your element.” Xor whispers. 

Ysurei stops, still as stone. Hide our element? They want us to hide our element? The pride and joy of being an Elemental, hidden beneath our skin? My heart pounds as the insult echoes in my
mind. 

“I know, it’s a large ask.” Xor says, his voice low. “But it’s either that, assimilation, or… living as outlaws.”

I look towards Kael and Adva, both seeming just as speechless as me. That’s when it hits me. Gaea doesn’t view us as refugees. We’re volatile aliens with the power to harm their infrastructure, 
but the ability to propel their own society. We’re assets desperate for safety, easy to exploit. Gaea isn't a home, it’s a prison. 

